// mentl.mn — the teaching substrate: the gradient at a declaration, and the
// Synth effect a hole's proposals arrive through.
//
// Every aspect the cursor reads is a handler over GraphRead and EnvRead —
// many arms, one graph, the octopus's shape. What the medium offers the
// developer is proven before they see it: a candidate is bound inside a
// checkpoint, inferred and verified, and rolled back, so a proposal is a move
// the medium can prove and never a guess.
//
// A proposer is a handler on Synth — the enumerator the medium ships, or a
// tool someone installs over it — and the compiler verifies whichever one
// proposed.
import types

import choice

import driver

import effects

import format

import graph

import infer

import query

// Mentl-owned ADTs (the Annotation a suggestion carries lives in types.mn,
// beside the suggestion that carries it)
//
// What a proven ABSENCE guarantees, named for the developer: a lock's label.
// Each is a guarantee the row proves and a caller may rely on, never an
// optimization the medium performs. The vocabulary once named five the
// medium never does — memoization, compile-time evaluation, automatic
// parallelization, bounds-check elimination and zero-copy transfer — and
// the gradient ranked by COUNTING them, so `with Pure` won every tie on the
// strength of three promises nothing kept (D4, 2026-10-02).
type Capability
  // !Alloc: a real-time callback may call it
  = CRealTime
  // !IO: it touches no file and no console
  | CIsolated
  // !Network: it reaches no network
  | CSandbox
  // !Trap: no partial primitive it reaches can fail. Not "total": a function
  // that never returns is `!Trap` too, and termination is in no row
  // (`Hβ.effects.divergence-is-a-row-fact`); totality is the conjunction of
  // `!Trap` with a proven `!Diverge`, which that peer names.
  | CTrapFree
  // Pure: it performs nothing at all
  | CPure
  // !E for an effect with no domain meaning: the absence itself
  | CProvenAbsence(String)

// The propose projection is `Proposal` (types.mn), beside the `Domain` its
// value question carries: one VERDICT plus every vocabulary candidate the
// ALLOWED row refused, each refusal keeping its Why (PLAN §5: the row pruning
// IS the second moat, and a pruned candidate the developer cannot see is a
// guess they cannot audit).
type Context = Context([(String, Binding, Reason, SchemeKind, Int)], EffRow, Option(Int), Int)

// scope bindings (the Env's own entries), the LIVE active-effect row, the enclosing declaration (None at top level — the vocabulary excludes every declaration whose references reach it: proposing the hole's own enclosing fn, or anything that transitively calls it, is nontermination by construction), and the HOLE'S OWN HANDLE — the cell the proposals are for. The fourth field was the hole's SPAN, a coordinate copied beside the very handle that gives it live, and the copy was load-bearing in the worst way: with only a span the proof gate had no cell to bind, so it instantiated a FRESH target and called that safety. The handle carries the span (graph_span_of), the type (ty_of_kind over its live kind), and the union-find root every candidate must agree with. One edge where three copies were.
// The full Boolean row reaches synth — not a
// flattened positive [EffName]. So `!E` (the
// negative capability, PLAN §4⑥) is readable at
// the synth gate: the row's ABSENT field IS the
// forbidden set (`let EfRow(_, a, _) = row`),
// carried on the one row the graph already
// bound. The cursor carries the row handle,
// never a lossy name-list re-derived from it
// (Carried-Truth).
//
// Teach effect — the gradient and the Why, the two tentacles the cursor asks
// of the substrate at a position. The effect once carried three more — a
// hover accumulator, an error-catalog lookup and an unlock preview — that
// nothing in the medium ever performed (`mentl query src/main.mn
// unreferenced` named all three), with a handler arm and a catalog of
// summaries behind them; they are deleted with D4 rather than kept as
// promises.
effect Teach {
  teach_gradient(Int) -> Teaching
  // the pins a declaration's head authors that the judgment derives, every
  // one proven lifted together — the read `mentl tighten` writes from
  teach_lifts(Int) -> [Lift]
  teach_why(Int) -> [Reason]
}

// ═══ Synth effect — proposers as handlers ══════════════════════════  // drift-audit: ignore — D.1.R reserved block; decoration removal is a separate sub-handle
// Any proposer — enumerative, SMT, LLM, user — plugs in as a handler; the
// compiler verifies, inside the proposer's own segment walk.
effect Synth {
  // the verdict with its Why + row-rejected vocabulary with theirs.
  // The target TYPE is not a parameter: the Context carries the hole's
  // handle, and a type read from that handle is the same fact one
  // dereference away. Passing it separately was a copy that could drift
  // from the cell it described — and did, for exactly as long as the
  // proof gate compared candidates against it instead of against the cell.
  synth_propose(EffRow, Context) -> Proposal
}

// Default Teach handler — the substrate's self-projection, reading the graph
// through GraphRead: the gradient by the bracket that proves each annotation,
// the Why by the reason DAG. Stateless: the link-wide census of performed
// names it held as state was the lock vocabulary, and a lock is read off the
// clauses that stand on the declaration (T2, 2026-10-13).
handler mentl_default {
  teach_gradient(handle) => resume(gradient_next(handle)),
  teach_lifts(handle) => resume(lifts_at(handle)),
  teach_why(handle) => resume(why_from_handle(handle)),
}

// THE GRADIENT AT A DECLARATION (D4, 2026-10-02). Every annotation the
// gradient proposes is PROVEN in a checkpoint bracket by the judgment's own
// functions — the row gate's subsumption for a clause, the ledger's own
// decision for a precondition's obligations and the call site's own claim
// for its callers, the authored return's own claim for a postcondition — and
// its leverage is what the bracket measured. Until D4 the gradient proposed
// effect clauses alone, so at `fn inv(n) = 100 / n`, whose one unproven fact
// is that `n` may be zero, it answered `with !Alloc`; the refinement that
// makes the division total was promised by SYNTAX's partiality section and
// had no constructor.
//
// The positions a declaration offers: each parameter its open obligations
// lean on, the return when callers' open claims rest on it, and the row
// clause while no row is authored (a row the author wrote is either tight or
// proven over-declared, and that proof — T_OverDeclared, banked for `mentl
// tighten` — is what the render layer reads instead).
fn gradient_next(handle) = match gradient_decl(handle) {
  Some(d) => decl_teaching(d),
  None => TeachNone,
}

// The lifts alone, for the verb that writes every head of a project.
fn lifts_at(handle) = match gradient_decl(handle) {
  Some(Decl(f)) => lifts_of(f),
  None => [],
}

// The declaration the gradient reads at a node: a function declaration the
// judgment bound to a function type, or nothing.
fn gradient_decl(handle) = match graph_node_body(handle) {
  NStmt(FnStmt(name, params, retty, effs, body)) => match graph_chase(handle) {
    GNode(NBound(ty), anchor) => match ty {
      TFun(_, ret, _) => Some(Decl({anchor, body, effs, handle, name, params, ret, retty, ty})),
      TInt | TFloat | TUnit | TByte | TAddr | TList(_) | TTuple(_) | TVar(_) | TName(_, _) | TRecord(_)
      | TRecordOpen(_, _) | TRefined(_, _) | TReprPin(_, _) | TCont(_, _, _, _) | TAlias(_, _) | TValue(_) => None,
    },
    GNode(_, _) => None,
  },
  NExpr(_) | NStmt(_) | NPat(_) | NHole(_) | NModule(_) | NLink(_) | NTypeAnn(_) | NName(_)
  | NRow(_) | NParam(_) => None,
}

// One declaration as the gradient reads it: its handle and name, the
// signature it was written with, the type it was judged at, and the reason
// its suggestions anchor to.
type Decl = {
  anchor: Reason,
  body: Node,
  effs: [(RowTerm, Bool, EConn)],
  handle: Int,
  name: String,
  params: [Node],
  ret: Ty,
  retty: Node,
  ty: Ty,
}

fn decl_teaching(d) = match d {
  Decl(f) => {
    let owed = open_obligations(f.handle)
    let rows = if len(f.effs) > 0 { [] } else { row_suggestions(f) }
    let proven = precondition_suggestions(f, owed) ++ return_suggestions(f) ++ rows
    match rank_suggestions(proven) {
      [] => need_or_lift(f, owed),
      [best, ...rest] => if lev_discharged(best) == 0 && has_need(f, owed) {
        need_or_lift(f, owed)
      } else {
        teaching_from(best, rest)
      },
    }
  },
}

// ── The locks something stands on (T2, 2026-10-13) ────────────────────
//
// A clause another declaration authored is a proof resting on this
// declaration's row: `fn g() with !Alloc = f()` holds because f allocates
// nothing, so an allocation written into f later is refused at g, far from
// the line that made it. The lock teaches f the clause g stands on, and the
// refusal moves to f. A declaration no clause reaches has nothing standing
// on its absences, and no lock is taught there. Before 2026-10-13 every
// declaration whose row lacked an effect the link performed was offered the
// strongest clause it proved — 367 of 427 declarations across six wheel
// modules on 2026-10-06 — and `fn main() = 42` was told to add `with Pure`.
//
// The dependents are read along the application edge — the declaration's
// in-edges that are calls (`graph_applies_of`) — and up through every
// caller, claused or not: g's `!Alloc` stands on f through an unclaused h,
// and k's `!Trap` stands on f through a claused g. A `~>` install between
// the application and the clause absorbs what its handler answers, and the
// clause stands on nothing of the row for those effects. An instance
// negation (`!Sample(44100)`) is band A's precision and is not yet a lock.
fn standing_clauses(decl, seen) = graph_applies_of(decl) |> flat_map(standing_at(seen, ??))

fn standing_at(seen, app_h) = match enclosing_decl(app_h) {
  None => [],
  Some(dh) => match decl_clause(dh) {
    None => [],
    Some((dname, effs)) => if any({ s => s == dh }, seen) {
      []
    } else {
      let masked = masked_between(app_h, dh)
      let here = clause_locks(effs) |> filter(clause_holds_at(dh, ??)) |> map(stood_by(dname, ??))
      here ++ standing_clauses(dh, push(seen, dh)) |> filter(lock_unmasked(masked, ??))
    },
  },
}

fn stood_by(dname: String, ann) = (ann, dname)

// A clause stands on its callees' rows only while it holds: a declaration
// whose row already carries the effect its clause negates is refused for it,
// and stands on nothing of it. A `!WASI` caller handing a printing callback
// to a higher-order function was taught to rest on that function's absence
// of WASI, which the function does not have once the callback performs it.
fn clause_holds_at(dh, ann) = match resolve_row(extract_row(chase_ty(TVar(dh)))) {
  EfRow(present, _, _) => match ann {
    ANotEffect(name, _) => !any({ p => eff_name_str(p) == name }, present),
    APure(_) => !any(effect_named, present),
    ARefined(_) => false,
    AReturns(_) => false,
  },
}

fn effect_named(p) = match p {
  ENamed(_) | EParameterized(_, _) => true,
  EPure => false,
}

// A declaration's name and authored clause — a function's or a handler's.
fn decl_clause(dh) = match graph_node_body(dh) {
  NStmt(FnStmt(name, _, _, effs, _)) => Some((name, effs)),
  NStmt(HandlerDeclStmt(name, _, _, _, _, effs)) => Some((name, effs)),
  NExpr(_) | NStmt(_) | NPat(_) | NHole(_) | NModule(_) | NLink(_) | NTypeAnn(_) | NName(_)
  | NRow(_) | NParam(_) => None,
}

// The locks a clause states: `with Pure`, or each bare name it negates — a
// group's members under the group's own sign.
fn clause_locks(effs) =
  if has_pure_declared(effs) { [APure(None)] } else { effs |> flat_map(term_locks(false, ??)) }

fn term_locks(flipped, term) = match term {
  (RtEff(n, _), neg, _) => match n {
    ENamed(_) => if neg != flipped { [ANotEffect(eff_name_str(n), None)] } else { [] },
    EParameterized(_, _) => [],
    EPure => [],
  },
  (RtGroup(ts), neg, _) => ts |> flat_map(term_locks(neg != flipped, ??)),
}

// What the installs between an application and its declaration absorb: at
// each `~>` whose body holds the application, the effects its handler's arms
// answer.
fn masked_between(child, top) = if child == top {
  []
} else {
  match graph_parent_of(child) {
    None => [],
    Some(p) => match graph_node_body(p) {
      NExpr(PipeExpr(PTee, l, r)) => if l == child { tee_absorbs(r) ++ masked_between(p, top) } else { masked_between(p, top) },
      NExpr(_) | NStmt(_) | NPat(_) | NHole(_) | NModule(_) | NLink(_) | NTypeAnn(_) | NName(_)
      | NRow(_) | NParam(_) => masked_between(p, top),
    },
  }
}

fn tee_absorbs(r) = match tee_arms(r) {
  Some(arms) => handler_effect_names(arms),
  None => [],
}

fn lock_unmasked(masked, pair) = match pair {
  (ann, _) => match ann {
    ANotEffect(name, _) => !string_in_list(masked, name),
    APure(_) => len(masked) == 0,
    ARefined(_) => false,
    AReturns(_) => false,
  },
}

// One candidate per lock, with every declaration that stands on it: `with
// Pure` first, then the negations as the walk found them.
fn lock_candidates(pairs) = {
  let grouped = fold([], add_dependent, pairs)
  filter(is_pure_lock, grouped) ++ filter({ c => !is_pure_lock(c) }, grouped)
}

fn add_dependent(acc, pair) = match pair {
  (ann, dname) => if any(same_lock(ann, ??), acc) {
    map(join_dependent(ann, dname, ??), acc)
  } else {
    push(acc, (ann, [dname]))
  },
}

fn join_dependent(ann, dname: String, c) = match c {
  (a, ds) => if lock_eq(a, ann) && !string_in_list(ds, dname) { (a, push(ds, dname)) } else { c },
}

fn same_lock(ann, c) = match c {
  (a, _) => lock_eq(a, ann),
}

fn is_pure_lock(c) = match c {
  (a, _) => match a {
    APure(_) => true,
    ANotEffect(_, _) => false,
    ARefined(_) => false,
    AReturns(_) => false,
  },
}

fn lock_eq(a, b) = match a {
  APure(_) => match b {
    APure(_) => true,
    ANotEffect(_, _) => false,
    ARefined(_) => false,
    AReturns(_) => false,
  },
  ANotEffect(x, _) => match b {
    ANotEffect(y, _) => x == y,
    APure(_) => false,
    ARefined(_) => false,
    AReturns(_) => false,
  },
  ARefined(_) => false,
  AReturns(_) => false,
}

// Map an Annotation variant to its SuggestionKind axis. Drift 8
// closure: SuggestionKind is the tagged dispatch surface; the
// match below is the only place the mapping lives.
fn suggestion_kind_of(ann) with Pure = match ann {
  APure(_) => SuggestEffectRow,
  ANotEffect(_, _) => SuggestEffectRow,
  ARefined(_) => SuggestRefinement,
  AReturns(_) => SuggestReturnType,
}

// Capability ADT to display label list. Used by AnnotationSuggestion.unlocks.
// Composition over `show_capability` (already in mentl.mn).
fn capability_labels(caps) = map(show_capability, caps)

// Suggestion's Reason edge. Wraps the candidate's anchor reason in a
// Located/Unmarked chain that the Why-tentacle walks. Span comes from
// the annotation's source-span field when present (user-originated
// candidate), span_zero otherwise (fully internal speculation).
fn suggestion_reason(ann) = {
  let span = unwrap_source_span(annotation_source_span(ann))
  Located(span, Inferred(suggestion_kind_label(suggestion_kind_of(ann))))
}

fn annotation_source_span(ann) with Pure = match ann {
  APure(sp) => sp,
  ANotEffect(_, sp) => sp,
  ARefined(_) => None,
  AReturns(_) => None,
}

fn suggestion_kind_label(k) with Pure = match k {
  SuggestEffectRow => "gradient: effect-row tightening",
  SuggestRefinement => "gradient: a parameter's precondition",
  SuggestReturnType => "gradient: a return contract",
}

// A proven annotation and the position it annotates. The alternatives a
// question chooses between share one position: a parameter, or the return.
// Row clauses at one declaration are independent promises, never
// alternatives — `!Alloc` does not exclude `!IO`.
type Site = SiteParam(Int) | SiteReturn | SiteRow

type Proven = Proven(Site, AnnotationSuggestion)

// ── The row clause: a lock some clause stands on, proven ───────────────
//
// Each candidate is a lock a dependent's clause stands on (above), proven by
// the declaration gate's own engine in a fork of its own (`speculate`): the
// narrowed row bound, the body's row asked to subsume it, undone. A proven
// clause locks an absence the body already has — the gate every edit of the
// body must meet — and discharges nothing.
fn row_suggestions(f) = {
  let candidates = standing_clauses(f.handle, [f.handle]) |> lock_candidates
  let held = {
    let c = choose(candidates)
    if lock_holds(f.handle, f.ty, c) { [c] } else { [] }
  } ~> speculate ~> judgment_core
  held |> map(row_suggestion)
}

fn lock_holds(handle, base_ty, c) = match c {
  (ann, _) => apply_annotation_tentatively(handle, ann, base_ty) && verify_after_apply(handle),
}

// A lock's leverage: the guarantees it names, and the declarations whose
// clauses stand on it.
fn row_suggestion(c) = match c {
  (ann, stands) => Proven(
    SiteRow,
    AnnotationSuggestion(
      ann,
      Leverage({
        discharged: 0,
        locks: capability_labels(capabilities_for_annotation(ann)),
        opened: 0,
        refuted: 0,
        safe: [],
        stands,
      }),
      suggestion_reason(ann)
    )
  ),
}

// ── The open obligations a declaration owes ────────────────────────────
//
// The ledger's open claims raised inside the declaration's extent IN ITS
// OWN MODULE — the obligation carries its module, and every module numbers
// its lines from 1, so an extent alone matches a stranger's claim at the
// same lines. A claim pending over an authored hole is the hole's demand,
// paid by its fill, and is the proposer's to answer.
fn open_obligations(h) = verify_debt() |> filter(owed_at(graph_module_of(h), graph_span_of(h), ??))

fn owed_at(m, sp, o) = match o {
  (dm, dsp, pred, _) => dm == m && span_overlaps(sp, dsp) && !claim_is_a_holes_demand(pred),
}

// ── The precondition: an open obligation discharged ────────────────────
//
// Each open obligation names the parameters its value leans on that state no
// precondition yet; each such parameter of THIS declaration is a position,
// and every refinement alias the declaration's module reaches whose base is
// the parameter's type is a candidate there. The search is guided by the
// claim: a parameter no open obligation reads is never tried.
fn precondition_suggestions(f, owed) = {
  let aliases = aliases_in_reach(graph_module_of(f.handle))
  owed |> flat_map(own_leans(f, ??)) |> fold([], add_lean, ??) |> flat_map(param_candidates(f, owed, aliases, ??))
}

// The leans of an obligation that are this declaration's own parameters,
// each with its position — matched by identity, never by name: a declared
// parameter's fact carries the guard cell its declaration's signature
// carries at that position. An obligation raised here can lean on another
// declaration's parameter — a callee's, through a crossing the claim could
// not resolve: a refined function handed to an applier leaves the claim on
// the applier's own parameter (tests/teach/need-is-own.mn) — or on a
// lambda's inside the body, and neither is a position this declaration's
// head can state.
fn own_leans(f, o) = match o {
  (_, _, pred, _) => predicate_leans(pred) |> flat_map(own_lean(f, ??)),
}

fn own_lean(f, lean) = match lean {
  (b, name) => match f.ty {
    TFun(pps, _, _) => match graph_binder_of(b) {
      Some(fact) => match fact {
        ParamFact(pf) => pps |> enumerate |> filter(guarded_by(pf.guard, ??)) |> map(lean_at_slot(b, name, ??)),
        PartFact(_) => [],
        StateFact(_) => [],
      },
      None => [],
    },
    TInt | TFloat | TUnit | TByte | TAddr | TList(_) | TTuple(_) | TVar(_) | TName(_, _) | TRecord(_)
    | TRecordOpen(_, _) | TRefined(_, _) | TReprPin(_, _) | TCont(_, _, _, _) | TAlias(_, _) | TValue(_) => [],
  },
}

fn guarded_by(g, ip) = match ip {
  (_, p) => match param_row(p) {
    EfRow(_, _, EtOpen(edges)) => any(edge_is(g, ??), edges),
    EfRow(_, _, _) => false,
  },
}

fn edge_is(g, e) = edge_cell(e) == g

fn lean_at_slot(b, name: String, ip) = match ip {
  (i, _) => (b, name, i),
}

fn predicate_leans(pred) = match pred {
  PTotalDiv(_, a, b) => value_leans(b) ++ value_leans(a),
  PInBounds(recv, idx) => value_leans(idx) ++ value_leans(recv),
  PWithSelf(h, _) => value_leans(h),
  PTrue | PCompare(_, _, _) | PAnd(_, _) | POr(_, _) | PNot(_) | PBoolNode(_) | PPatternMatch(_, _)
  | PProvides(_) => [],
}

// The parameters a value leans on that state no precondition, each with the
// name a use reads it by: through a reference's own link (the value walk's
// edge, never the unification class), a let's value, an arithmetic operand,
// a join's tails. A call's result leans on its callee's contract, never on
// what was handed to it, so a call ends the walk. A parameter with a
// precondition — stated or learned — already has one; a parameter written
// with a plain type leans like an unannotated one, and a refinement over it
// is written only where the alias's base IS that type (`writable_over`), so
// the name the developer wrote survives as the base the alias declares.
fn value_leans(h) = match join_tails(h) {
  Some(tails) => tails |> flat_map(value_leans),
  None => match graph_node_body(h) {
    NExpr(VarRef(name)) => if name == "self" { [] } else { link_leans(h, name) },
    NExpr(BinOpExpr(_, l, r)) => value_leans(l) ++ value_leans(r),
    NExpr(UnaryOpExpr(_, e)) => value_leans(e),
    NExpr(_) | NStmt(_) | NPat(_) | NHole(_) | NModule(_) | NLink(_) | NTypeAnn(_) | NName(_)
    | NRow(_) | NParam(_) => [],
  },
}

fn link_leans(use_h, name: String) = match binder_read_by(use_h) {
  None => [],
  Some(b) => match graph_binder_of(b) {
    Some(fact) => match fact {
      ParamFact(pf) => match pf.pre {
        Unstated => [(b, name)],
        Unrefined => [(b, name)],
        Stated(_) => [],
        Learned(_) => [],
      },
      PartFact(_) => [],
      StateFact(_) => [],
    },
    None => match graph_node_body(b) {
      NExpr(_) => value_leans(b),
      NStmt(_) | NPat(_) | NHole(_) | NModule(_) | NLink(_) | NTypeAnn(_) | NName(_) | NRow(_) | NParam(_) => [],
    },
  },
}

fn add_lean(acc, lean) = match lean {
  (b, _, _) => if any(lean_at(b, ??), acc) { acc } else { push(acc, lean) },
}

fn lean_at(b, lean) = match lean {
  (x, _, _) => x == b,
}

// The refinement vocabulary a declaration's module can name: every `type X
// = Base where pred` in a module it reaches through its imports (the
// prelude's ambient closure included), each with the contract the env
// registered for it — the Ty a written annotation resolves to, so the
// bracket proves exactly what an accepted one would. A type the module does
// not reach would be refused at the reference (E_MissingImport); the medium
// never proposes it, and it never proposes a type no one declared.
fn aliases_in_reach(mh) = module_reach_of(mh) |> flat_map(module_refinements)

fn module_refinements(m) = match graph_node_body(m) {
  NModule(mod) => mod.decls |> flat_map(refinement_alias),
  NExpr(_) | NStmt(_) | NPat(_) | NHole(_) | NLink(_) | NTypeAnn(_) | NName(_) | NRow(_) | NParam(_) => [],
}

fn refinement_alias(d) = match graph_node_body(d) {
  NStmt(RefineStmt(name, _, _)) => match env_lookup(name) {
    Some(entry) => match entry {
      (scheme, _, _) => [(name, judgment_ty(scheme))],
    },
    None => [],
  },
  NExpr(_) | NStmt(_) | NPat(_) | NHole(_) | NModule(_) | NLink(_) | NTypeAnn(_) | NName(_)
  | NRow(_) | NParam(_) => [],
}

fn param_candidates(f, owed, aliases, lean) = match lean {
  (b, name, i) => {
    let fitting =
      aliases |> filter(binder_fits(b, ??)) |> filter(writable_over(written_param_ty(list_index(f.params, i)), ??))
    let counted = {
      let alias = choose(fitting)
      [(alias, precondition_counts(f, owed, b, i, alias))]
    } ~> speculate ~> judgment_core
    counted |> flat_map(precondition_proven(i, name, ??))
  },
}

// What a parameter's author wrote as its type: the annotation's type, or the
// parameter's own cell where none was written.
fn written_param_ty(p) = match param_ann(p) {
  Some(a) => ann_ty_of(a),
  None => TVar(p),
}

// A refinement can be written over what the parameter says: nothing, or a
// word type the alias refines — written `n: Int`, the accept writes `n:
// NonZero`, and `Int` survives as the base the alias declares, which is what
// the head's census counts it as (cursor_transport.mn's stated_by). Over any
// other written type the accept would drop a name the developer chose, so it
// is no candidate.
fn writable_over(written, alias) = match unwritten_cell(written) {
  Some(_) => true,
  None => is_word_base(written) && alias_fits(written, alias),
}

fn is_word_base(t) = match t {
  TInt => true,
  TFloat => true,
  TByte => true,
  TUnit | TAddr | TList(_) | TTuple(_) | TFun(_, _, _) | TVar(_) | TName(_, _) | TRecord(_)
  | TRecordOpen(_, _) | TRefined(_, _) | TReprPin(_, _) | TCont(_, _, _, _) | TAlias(_, _) | TValue(_) => false,
}

fn binder_fits(b, alias) = match graph_chase(b) {
  GNode(NBound(t), _) => alias_fits(t, alias),
  GNode(_, _) => false,
}

fn alias_fits(t, alias) = match alias {
  (_, contract) => same_base(base_of(contract), base_of(t)),
}

fn base_of(t) = match t {
  TAlias(_, inner) => base_of(inner),
  TRefined(b, _) => base_of(b),
  TReprPin(_, b) => base_of(b),
  TInt | TFloat | TUnit | TByte | TAddr | TList(_) | TTuple(_) | TFun(_, _, _) | TVar(_)
  | TName(_, _) | TRecord(_) | TRecordOpen(_, _) | TCont(_, _, _, _) | TValue(_) => t,
}

fn same_base(a, b) = match a {
  TInt => match b {
    TInt => true,
    TFloat | TUnit | TByte | TAddr | TList(_) | TTuple(_) | TFun(_, _, _) | TVar(_) | TName(_, _)
    | TRecord(_) | TRecordOpen(_, _) | TRefined(_, _) | TReprPin(_, _) | TCont(_, _, _, _)
    | TAlias(_, _) | TValue(_) => false,
  },
  TFloat => match b {
    TFloat => true,
    TInt | TUnit | TByte | TAddr | TList(_) | TTuple(_) | TFun(_, _, _) | TVar(_) | TName(_, _)
    | TRecord(_) | TRecordOpen(_, _) | TRefined(_, _) | TReprPin(_, _) | TCont(_, _, _, _)
    | TAlias(_, _) | TValue(_) => false,
  },
  TByte => match b {
    TByte => true,
    TInt | TFloat | TUnit | TAddr | TList(_) | TTuple(_) | TFun(_, _, _) | TVar(_) | TName(_, _)
    | TRecord(_) | TRecordOpen(_, _) | TRefined(_, _) | TReprPin(_, _) | TCont(_, _, _, _)
    | TAlias(_, _) | TValue(_) => false,
  },
  TUnit | TAddr | TList(_) | TTuple(_) | TFun(_, _, _) | TVar(_) | TName(_, _) | TRecord(_)
  | TRecordOpen(_, _) | TRefined(_, _) | TReprPin(_, _) | TCont(_, _, _, _) | TAlias(_, _) | TValue(_) => false,
}

// THE PRECONDITION'S BRACKET. The candidate is stated on the parameter as an
// authored refinement states it (its binder fact's precondition), and the
// judgment's own decisions run against it: every open obligation of the
// declaration through the ledger's decision, and every application of the
// declaration the judgment drew through the call site's own two steps
// (`dry_claim`). Counted on a trail of their own (`speculate`), undone,
// built after. An application the edge does not hold — a partial's supplied
// argument, the declaration handed to another function as a value — is
// judged when the annotation is accepted, not counted here
// (`Hβ.teach.leverage-reads-direct-applications`).
//
// A candidate that refutes a body claim is no precondition; one that
// discharges nothing is no gain. A REFUTED CALL is reported, not refused:
// under the requirement the body states, it is a call that breaks it — the
// program's own defect — and a stronger candidate refuting more calls is the
// one asking more than the body needs.
fn precondition_counts(f, owed, b, i, alias) = match alias {
  (_, contract) => {
    restate_param(b, contract)
    let paid = owed |> filter(obligation_holds)
    let broken = owed |> filter(obligation_refuted) |> len
    let verdicts =
      latest_applications(graph_applies_of(f.handle)) |> map(claim_at_call(contract, i, ??))
    (
      len(paid),
      broken,
      verdicts |> filter(verdict_refuted) |> len,
      verdicts |> filter(verdict_open) |> len,
      trap_leaves(f, paid),
      map(made_safe, paid)
    )
  },
}

fn precondition_proven(i, name: String, counted) = match counted {
  ((aname, contract), (discharged, broken, refuted, opened, trap_free, safe)) => if discharged == 0 || broken > 0 {
    []
  } else {
    let ann = ARefined({alias: aname, contract, param: name})
    [
      Proven(
        SiteParam(i),
        AnnotationSuggestion(
          ann,
          Leverage({discharged, locks: unlocked_labels(trap_free), opened, refuted, safe, stands: []}),
          suggestion_reason(ann)
        )
      )
    ]
  },
}

// What paying an obligation makes safe, in the developer's words: the
// instruction whose precondition the claim guards, else the claim itself.
fn made_safe(o) = match o {
  (_, _, pred, _) => match pred {
    PTotalDiv(_, _, _) => "the division cannot fail",
    PInBounds(_, _) => "the index stays in bounds",
    PWithSelf(_, _) | PTrue | PCompare(_, _, _) | PAnd(_, _) | POr(_, _) | PNot(_) | PBoolNode(_)
    | PPatternMatch(_, _) | PProvides(_) => "the claim holds",
  },
}

// WHAT THE DEBT PAID UNLOCKS. A precondition that pays every open claim Trap
// enters the declaration by takes Trap off its row — `!Trap` becomes a clause
// the body proves — and the voice says so beside the debt, in the developer's
// words: the guarantee the annotation buys, never the count alone. Trap
// leaves when it enters at all and every chain it enters by ends at a site
// whose claim the candidate pays, so nothing else charges it; a chain through
// a callee's open claim keeps it.
fn trap_leaves(f, paid) = match effect_entries_of(f.handle) |> filter(chain_enters("Trap", ??)) {
  [] => false,
  chains => all(chain_paid(paid, ??), chains),
}

fn chain_paid(paid, chain) = chain_sites(chain) |> any({ site => any(pays(site, ??), paid) })

fn pays(site, o) = match o {
  (_, sp, _, _) => span_overlaps(graph_span_of(site), sp),
}

// The site a chain ends at — the construct that charges the effect itself —
// as a list, empty for a reason that is no chain.
fn chain_sites(chain) = match chain {
  EffectEnters(eff, site, next) => if chain_enters(eff, next) { chain_sites(next) } else { [site] },
  Declared(_) | Inferred(_) | Fresh(_) | OpConstraint(_, _, _) | VarLookup(_, _) | FnReturn(_, _)
  | FnParam(_, _, _) | MatchBranch(_, _) | ListElement(_) | IfBranch(_) | LetBinding(_, _)
  | Unified(_, _) | Instantiation(_, _) | Placeholder(_) | MissingVar(_) | Refinement(_, _)
  | Located(_, _) | InferredCallReturn(_, _) | InferredPipeResult(_, _) | DefaultReason(_, _)
  | Accepted(_, _, _) | LabelEnters(_, _, _) | ChainInstall(_, _) | StateBinding(_, _) => [],
}

fn unlocked_labels(trap_free) = if trap_free { capability_labels([CTrapFree]) } else { [] }

fn restate_param(b, contract) = match graph_binder_of(b) {
  Some(fact) => match fact {
    ParamFact(pf) => graph_binder_note(b, ParamFact({guard: pf.guard, owes: pf.owes, pre: Stated(contract)})),
    PartFact(_) => (),
    StateFact(_) => (),
  },
  None => (),
}

fn obligation_holds(o) = match o {
  (_, _, pred, _) => verdict_holds(predicate_decide(pred)),
}

fn obligation_refuted(o) = match o {
  (_, _, pred, _) => verdict_refuted(predicate_decide(pred)),
}

fn verdict_holds(v) = match v {
  Some(held) => held,
  None => false,
}

fn verdict_refuted(v) = match v {
  Some(held) => !held,
  None => false,
}

fn verdict_open(v) = match v {
  Some(_) => false,
  None => true,
}

// The arguments an application hands the declaration, in its parameters'
// order: a call's positional product, a pipe's piped value.
fn application_args(h) = match graph_node_body(h) {
  NExpr(PipeExpr(PForward, left, _)) => [left],
  NExpr(_) | NStmt(_) | NPat(_) | NHole(_) | NModule(_) | NLink(_) | NTypeAnn(_) | NName(_)
  | NRow(_) | NParam(_) => match graph_product_at(h) {
    ProductOf(args, _, _) => args,
    NoProduct => [],
  },
}

// The callee node when it references the declaration `decl`, or none.
fn callee_naming(callee, decl) = match graph_ref_decl(callee) {
  Some(d) => if d == decl { [callee] } else { [] },
  None => [],
}

// A session that has re-derived holds one application node per generation
// at the same source extent; the latest mint is the live one. The identity
// is the WHOLE extent in its module — two files' line 4 are two calls, and
// a chain `x |> inv |> inv` holds two applications that begin together
// (a declaration's position is its start, cursor.mn's latest_generation;
// applications nest at one start).
fn latest_applications(apps) = apps |> filter(no_later_mint(apps, ??))

fn no_later_mint(apps, h) = !any(later_mint_of(h, ??), apps)

fn later_mint_of(h, h2) =
  h2 > h && graph_module_of(h2) == graph_module_of(h) && graph_span_of(h2) == graph_span_of(h)

// The claim an application raises at its argument under the candidate — the
// call site's own two steps (`discharge_arg`: an unannotated parameter
// handed straight in learns the precondition, then the claim) — answered by
// a scratch ledger, its refutation narrated to no one.
fn claim_at_call(contract, i, app_h) = {
  let args = application_args(app_h)
  if i < len(args) { dry_claim(contract, args[i]) } else { Some(true) }
}

fn dry_claim(contract, arg) =
  learn_then_claim(contract, arg, graph_span_of(arg)) ~> verify_ledger ~> diag_quiet

fn learn_then_claim(contract, ah, sp: ValidSpan) = {
  learn_precondition(ah, contract)
  claim_refinement(contract, ah, sp)
}

// ── The return contract: callers' open claims discharged ───────────────
//
// A caller's open claim that rests on this declaration's result — a
// refinement demanded of a call to it — is a claim a stated return lets
// stand: a claim may stand on a contract its callee owes (P0), never a row
// fact, so a partiality claim is no reason to propose one. Only where such a
// claim exists is the return a position, and only while the declaration
// authored no return.
fn return_suggestions(f) = if authored_return(f.retty) {
  []
} else {
  match verify_debt() |> flat_map(resting_on(f.handle, ??)) {
    [] => [],
    resting => {
      let fitting = aliases_in_reach(graph_module_of(f.handle)) |> filter(ret_fits(f.ret, ??))
      let counted = {
        let alias = choose(fitting)
        [(alias, return_counts(f, resting, alias))]
      } ~> speculate ~> judgment_core
      counted |> flat_map(return_proven)
    },
  }
}

fn authored_return(retty) = match graph_node_body(retty) {
  NTypeAnn(_) => true,
  NExpr(_) | NStmt(_) | NPat(_) | NHole(_) | NModule(_) | NLink(_) | NName(_) | NRow(_) | NParam(_) => false,
}

fn ret_fits(ret, alias) = alias_fits(chase_deep(ret), alias)

fn resting_on(decl, o) = match o {
  (_, _, pred, _) => match pred {
    PWithSelf(h, _) => match value_calls(h, decl) {
      [] => [],
      chs => [(pred, chs)],
    },
    PTrue | PCompare(_, _, _) | PAnd(_, _) | POr(_, _) | PNot(_) | PBoolNode(_) | PPatternMatch(_, _)
    | PTotalDiv(_, _, _) | PProvides(_) | PInBounds(_, _) => [],
  },
}

// The callee nodes through which a value IS a call to the declaration: the
// call, a pipe stage naming it, a let of either, a join's tails.
fn value_calls(h, decl) = match join_tails(h) {
  Some(tails) => tails |> flat_map(value_calls(??, decl)),
  None => match graph_node_body(h) {
    NExpr(CallExpr(callee, _)) => callee_naming(callee, decl),
    NExpr(PipeExpr(PForward, _, stage)) => callee_naming(stage, decl),
    NExpr(VarRef(_)) => match binder_read_by(h) {
      Some(b) => match graph_binder_of(b) {
        Some(_) => [],
        None => value_calls(b, decl),
      },
      None => [],
    },
    NExpr(_) | NStmt(_) | NPat(_) | NHole(_) | NModule(_) | NLink(_) | NTypeAnn(_) | NName(_)
    | NRow(_) | NParam(_) => [],
  },
}

// THE RETURN'S BRACKET. The body must prove the contract as the authored
// return's own claim would (`claim_authored_return`: the claim alone, no
// learning); each resting claim is then re-decided with every call it rests
// on read as returning the contract — the edge `post_leaves` follows — and
// counted on a trail of their own (`speculate`), undone, built after.
fn return_counts(f, resting, alias) = match alias {
  (_, contract) => {
    let body_holds = verdict_holds(dry_return_claim(contract, f.body))
    resting |> each(rest_on_contract(contract, ??))
    (body_holds, resting |> filter(rest_holds) |> len, resting |> filter(rest_refuted) |> len)
  },
}

fn return_proven(counted) = match counted {
  ((aname, contract), (body_holds, discharged, refuted)) => if body_holds && discharged > 0 {
    let ann = AReturns({alias: aname, contract})
    [
      Proven(
        SiteReturn,
        AnnotationSuggestion(
          ann,
          Leverage({
            discharged,
            locks: [],
            opened: 0,
            refuted,
            safe: ["what its callers claim of the result holds"],
            stands: [],
          }),
          suggestion_reason(ann)
        )
      )
    ]
  } else {
    []
  },
}

fn dry_return_claim(contract, body) =
  claim_refinement(contract, body, graph_span_of(body)) ~> verify_ledger ~> diag_quiet

fn rest_on_contract(contract, r) = match r {
  (_, chs) => chs |> each(return_as(contract, ??)),
}

fn return_as(contract, ch) = match graph_chase(ch) {
  GNode(NBound(t), reason) => match t {
    TFun(ps, _, row) => graph_bind(ch, TFun(ps, contract, row), reason),
    TInt | TFloat | TUnit | TByte | TAddr | TList(_) | TTuple(_) | TVar(_) | TName(_, _) | TRecord(_)
    | TRecordOpen(_, _) | TRefined(_, _) | TReprPin(_, _) | TCont(_, _, _, _) | TAlias(_, _) | TValue(_) => (),
  },
  GNode(_, _) => (),
}

fn rest_holds(r) = match r {
  (pred, _) => verdict_holds(predicate_decide(pred)),
}

fn rest_refuted(r) = match r {
  (pred, _) => verdict_refuted(predicate_decide(pred)),
}

// THE ANNOTATION THE JUDGMENT DERIVES, LIFTED.
//
// A type written on a parameter or the return that the body alone decides is
// an inventory — the projected type written by hand, the row inventory's twin
// — and a type is authored at an intent boundary alone: a refinement, an
// ownership marker, a width (SYNTAX §«The Intent Boundary Rule for
// Parameters»). Whether the body decides it is a question only a judgment
// without the annotation answers, and the live cells cannot: they hold the
// written type whichever side decided it. So the candidate is the edit
// itself, judged in a fork — the head rendered without the annotation, the
// declaration read again from its lines and judged as an edit is
// (`rewritten_judged`), the type it is bound to compared with the live one up
// to renaming — and kept only where the face holds. The annotations are tried
// left to right, the return last, each over the lifts already held, so the
// set proposed was proven lifted together: `fn same(a: Int, b: Int) = a == b`
// lifts `a: Int`, which `b` still grounds, and keeps `b: Int`, since without
// both the declaration generalizes.
fn lifts_of(f) = written_types(f) |> fold([], lift_held(f, ??, ??))

fn lift_held(f, held, one) = if face_holds_without(f, held ++ [one]) { held ++ [one] } else { held }

// The types a head writes that are no intent boundary: each written parameter
// type and the written return, unless a refinement, a width or a row stands
// inside what was written. The boundary is read off the WRITTEN type, each
// name it spells resolved to what it declares, never off the cell the
// judgment bound: a parameter handed to a callee that demands the refinement
// learns it, so its cell holds the base alone while the head's `cutoff: Hz`
// is the author's decision to state the contract where the callee merely
// implies it (lesson 2's filter, measured 2026-10-27).
fn written_types(f) = flat_map(written_param, f.params) ++ written_return(f.retty)

fn written_param(p) = match param_ann(p) {
  Some(a) => if written_boundary(ann_ty_of(a)) { [] } else { [LiftParam(p)] },
  None => [],
}

fn written_return(retty) = match graph_node_body(retty) {
  NTypeAnn(t) => if written_boundary(t) { [] } else { [LiftReturn(retty)] },
  NExpr(_) | NStmt(_) | NPat(_) | NHole(_) | NModule(_) | NLink(_) | NName(_) | NRow(_) | NParam(_) => [],
}

fn written_boundary(t) = match t {
  // a name written in a type is a cell of its own whose node holds the name
  // (`written_cells`); the cell's binding is the base, so the node is read
  TVar(h) => match graph_node_body(h) {
    NTypeAnn(inner) => written_boundary(inner),
    NExpr(_) | NStmt(_) | NPat(_) | NHole(_) | NModule(_) | NLink(_) | NName(_) | NRow(_) | NParam(_) => false,
  },
  // the name resolves in the type namespace, as the judgment links it
  TName(n, args) => any(written_boundary, args) || match published_at(type_decl_named(n)) {
    Some((sch, _, _)) => declared_boundary(judgment_ty(sch)),
    None => false,
  },
  TRefined(_, _) | TReprPin(_, _) | TCont(_, _, _, _) => true,
  TAlias(_, inner) => written_boundary(inner),
  TList(e) => written_boundary(e),
  TTuple(es) => any(written_boundary, es),
  TRecord(fs) => any({ (_, ft) => written_boundary(ft) }, fs),
  TRecordOpen(fs, _) => any({ (_, ft) => written_boundary(ft) }, fs),
  TFun(ps, r, row) => any({ p => written_boundary(param_ty(p)) }, ps) || written_boundary(r) || row_authored(row),
  TInt | TFloat | TUnit | TByte | TAddr | TValue(_) => false,
}

// What a name declares, as a boundary: a refinement or a width anywhere in it,
// where a sum or a record type is a base the body may derive.
fn declared_boundary(t) = match t {
  TRefined(_, _) | TReprPin(_, _) | TCont(_, _, _, _) => true,
  TAlias(_, inner) => declared_boundary(inner),
  TList(e) => declared_boundary(e),
  TTuple(es) => any(declared_boundary, es),
  TRecord(fs) => any({ (_, ft) => declared_boundary(ft) }, fs),
  TRecordOpen(fs, _) => any({ (_, ft) => declared_boundary(ft) }, fs),
  TFun(ps, r, row) => any({ p => declared_boundary(param_ty(p)) }, ps) || declared_boundary(r) || row_authored(row),
  TInt | TFloat | TUnit | TByte | TAddr | TVar(_) | TName(_, _) | TValue(_) => false,
}

// A row written inside a type names an effect or negates one: a claim.
fn row_authored(row) = match row {
  EfRow(present, absent, _) => len(absent) > 0 || any(effect_named, present),
}

// The face holds without the pins when the declaration, read again from its
// module's text with the head rendered without them and judged in a fork of
// its own, is bound to a type alpha-equal to the live one. The head is spliced
// by the formatter's own rule, so a head the conservation census would refuse
// to write is never judged.
fn face_holds_without(f, lifted) = {
  let m = module_of(graph_module_of(f.handle))
  let head = lifted_head(f.name, f.params, f.retty, f.body, f.effs, lifted)
  match splice_decl_head(m.text, graph_span_of(f.handle), graph_span_of(f.body), head, lift_names(lifted)) {
    None => false,
    Some(text) => {
      let live = chase_deep(f.ty)
      let held = {
        let edited = choose([text])
        [fresh_face_holds(f, live, edited)]
      } ~> speculate ~> judgment_core
      any({ h => h }, held)
    },
  }
}

// In the fork: the edit judged, and the type the fresh declaration of the
// name is bound to — found in the statements read again, a nested declaration
// inside the one enclosing it — against the live face: alpha-equal, and
// rendered the same, since the alpha comparison reads a refinement by its base
// alone and the render names the alias.
fn fresh_face_holds(f, live, text) = match rewritten_judged(graph_module_of(f.handle), text) |> flat_map(decls_named(f.name, ??)) {
  [fresh, ..._] => match graph_chase(fresh) {
    GNode(NBound(ty), _) => {
      let face = chase_deep(ty)
      match ty_alpha_eq(face, live, []) {
        (held, _) => held && str_eq(show_type(face), show_type(live)),
      }
    },
    GNode(_, _) => false,
  },
  [] => false,
}

// The function declarations of a name in a statement's subtree.
fn decls_named(name: String, h) = match graph_node_body(h) {
  NStmt(FnStmt(n, _, _, _, _)) => if n == name { [h] } else { children_named(name, graph_node_body(h)) },
  body @ (
    NExpr(_) | NStmt(_) | NPat(_) | NHole(_) | NModule(_) | NLink(_) | NTypeAnn(_) | NName(_)
    | NRow(_) | NParam(_)
  ) => children_named(name, body),
}

fn children_named(name: String, body) = body_child_handles(body) |> flat_map(decls_named(name, ??))

// Every atom a lifted annotation spelled, as the conservation census keys it,
// so the splice admits their loss and nothing else's.
fn lift_names(lifted) = lifted |> flat_map(lift_written) |> flat_map(type_atoms)

fn lift_written(l) = match l {
  LiftParam(p) => match param_ann(p) {
    Some(a) => [ann_ty_of(a)],
    None => [],
  },
  LiftReturn(r) => [ann_ty_of(r)],
}

fn type_atoms(t) = lex(render_type_tokens(t)) |> flat_map(atom_keyed)

fn atom_keyed(tok) = match atom_key(tok) {
  Some(k) => [k],
  None => [],
}

// A lift as its head spelled it — `n: Int`, `-> Float` — the text the teach
// battery's contract names.
fn lift_text(l) = match l {
  LiftParam(p) => "{param_binder(p)}: {written_type(param_ann(p))}",
  LiftReturn(r) => "-> {render_type_tokens(ann_ty_of(r))}",
}

fn written_type(ann) = match ann {
  Some(a) => render_type_tokens(ann_ty_of(a)),
  None => "",
}

// ── The answer ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// THE RANKING: what a suggestion proves, read lexicographically — debt
// discharged first (the program becomes more proven), then fewer calls
// refuted and fewer left open (a precondition asking less of its callers),
// then the locks, the one more declarations stand on first. A stable
// insertion, so equal suggestions keep their enumeration order:
// preconditions in parameter order, the return, then `with Pure` and the
// negations as the dependents walk found them.
fn rank_suggestions(proven) = fold([], insert_ranked, proven)

fn insert_ranked(sorted, p) = match sorted {
  [] => [p],
  [q, ...rest] => if keys_less(proven_key(p), proven_key(q)) { [p] ++ sorted } else { [q] ++ insert_ranked(rest, p) },
}

fn proven_key(p) = match p {
  Proven(_, s) => match s {
    AnnotationSuggestion(_, lev, _) => match lev {
      Leverage(l) => [0 - l.discharged, l.refuted, l.opened, 0 - len(l.stands)],
    },
  },
}

fn keys_less(a, b) = match a {
  [] => false,
  [x, ...xs] => match b {
    [] => false,
    [y, ...ys] => if x < y { true } else if x > y { false } else { keys_less(xs, ys) },
  },
}

fn lev_discharged(p) = match p {
  Proven(_, s) => match s {
    AnnotationSuggestion(_, lev, _) => match lev {
      Leverage(l) => l.discharged,
    },
  },
}

// The answer from the ranking's head. A row clause is proposed outright. A
// precondition or a return contract tied with another candidate for the SAME
// position, proving and costing exactly the same, is a question: both are
// proven, they mean different things, and the program cannot choose.
fn teaching_from(best, rest) = match best {
  Proven(site, s) => match site {
    SiteRow => TeachAdd(s),
    SiteParam(_) | SiteReturn => match rest |> filter(same_choice(best, ??)) {
      [] => TeachAdd(s),
      ties => TeachAsk([s] ++ map(suggestion_of, ties)),
    },
  },
}

fn same_choice(a, b) = site_of(a) == site_of(b) && proven_key(a) == proven_key(b)

fn site_of(p) = match p {
  Proven(site, _) => site,
}

fn suggestion_of(p) = match p {
  Proven(_, s) => s,
}

// A NEED: an open obligation leaning on a parameter that states no
// precondition, where no alias the module reaches discharges it — the
// predicate the parameter must carry, said where it is owed. It outranks a
// lock: the unproven part of a declaration is where the next move is. Where
// nothing is owed and no clause stands on the row, the next move is the lift:
// the pins the judgment derives, which decide nothing.
fn need_or_lift(f, owed) = match owed |> flat_map(need_of(f, ??)) {
  [] => match lifts_of(f) {
    [] => TeachNone,
    lifts => TeachLift(lifts),
  },
  [n, ..._] => n,
}

fn has_need(f, owed) = len(owed |> flat_map(need_of(f, ??))) > 0

fn need_of(f, o) = match o {
  (_, sp, pred, _) => match own_leans(f, o) {
    [] => [],
    [lean, ..._] => match lean {
      (_, name, _) => [TeachNeed(name, pred, sp)],
    },
  },
}

// Speculative apply: compute the narrowed type/row, graph_bind it, and
// return the PROOF. For a row claim the proof is row_subsumes(body_row,
// narrowing) — the SAME engine the fn-finalize gate runs — read from
// base_ty's own row, the live body truth the graph already carries. The
// old contract returned unit and left "proven" to a post-bind boundness
// read; a bind always sticks, so an !Alloc claim on an allocating body
// read back proven (the boundness-only hole, seen RED on `++`-carrying
// main before this returned the subsumption).
fn apply_annotation_tentatively(handle, ann, base_ty) = match ann {
  APure(sp) => narrow_row_proven(handle, base_ty, mk_ef_pure(), sp),
  // The general severance: the negation-narrowing proof for ANY name
  // the link performs. The name is user-facing vocabulary at the one
  // mint; identity is the intern handle from here on (EffName-is-a-
  // handle LANDED — comparisons are word i32.eq by type).
  ANotEffect(name, sp) => narrow_row_proven(handle, base_ty, mk_ef_neg([ENamed(intern_str(name))]), sp),
  // A refinement is proven on its own trail against the obligations it
  // discharges (`precondition_counts`, `return_counts`); it narrows no row,
  // and a narrowing of its own would prove nothing it claims. Until D4 this
  // arm performed `verify` on a predicate no candidate ever supplied and
  // answered true, and the ownership arms answered true with no proof at
  // all — the reason ownership could never be offered under a ranking.
  ARefined(_) => false,
  AReturns(_) => false,
}

// A row NARROWING's apply + proof, one home: bind the narrowed TFun
// (trail-reverted on rollback) and return whether the body's own row —
// base_ty's row, the live truth inference already wrote — subsumes into
// the claim. row_subsumes is the fn-finalize gate's engine (the crown's
// by-name negation membership included), so "proven" here means exactly
// what the declaration gate would prove: with Pure demands the empty row,
// !E demands E provably absent.
fn narrow_row_proven(handle, base_ty, narrow, source_span) = match base_ty {
  TFun(_, _, base_row) => {
    narrow_row(handle, base_ty, narrow, source_span)
    row_subsumes(resolve_row(base_row), narrow)
  },
  TInt | TFloat | TUnit | TByte | TAddr | TList(_) | TTuple(_) | TVar(_) | TName(_, _) | TRecord(_)
  | TRecordOpen(_, _) | TRefined(_, _) | TReprPin(_, _) | TCont(_, _, _, _) | TAlias(_, _) | TValue(_) => false,
}

fn unwrap_source_span(opt) = match opt {
  Some(s) => s,
  None => span_zero(),
}

// What a proven clause guarantees, named. The list is what pipeline.mn's
// severance_named filters on and its render_module_severance spends: an
// effect this map does not name severs just as provably, and rides as its
// proven absence. A refinement guarantees no absence — its gain is the
// obligations it discharges, counted where it was proven.
fn capabilities_for_annotation(ann) = match ann {
  APure(_) => [CPure],
  // the known names keep their rich labels (capabilities_for_severance's
  // own mapping); an unmapped name unlocks its proven absence itself, the
  // crown's vocabulary as the capability.
  ANotEffect(name, _) => match capabilities_for_severance(ENamed(intern_str(name))) {
    [] => [CProvenAbsence(name)],
    known => known,
  },
  ARefined(_) => [],
  AReturns(_) => [],
}

// What adding `!E` to a fn that does not perform E guarantees, for the
// effects whose absence has a meaning beyond itself.
fn capabilities_for_severance(name) = match name {
  ENamed(h) => if h == intern_str("Alloc") {
    [CRealTime]
  } else if h == intern_str("IO") {
    [CIsolated]
  } else if h == intern_str("Network") {
    [CSandbox]
  } else if h == intern_str("Trap") {
    [CTrapFree]
  } else {
    []
  },
  EParameterized(_, _) => [],
  // the identity severs no capability
  EPure => [],
}

fn narrow_row(handle, base_ty, narrow, source_span) = match base_ty {
  TFun(params, ret, _) => {
    let narrowed = TFun(params, ret, narrow)
    // The trail reverts this bind on rollback. source_span is Some(s)
    // when the candidate originated at a user site (hovered hole,
    // quick-fix request); None for fully-internal speculation, in
    // which case we fall back to span_zero(). The Located wrapper
    // gives the Why Engine coordinates to render.
    graph_bind(
      handle,
      narrowed,
      Located(unwrap_source_span(source_span), Inferred("mentl speculative narrow"))
    )
  },
  TInt | TFloat | TUnit | TByte | TAddr | TList(_) | TTuple(_) | TVar(_) | TName(_, _) | TRecord(_)
  | TRecordOpen(_, _) | TRefined(_, _) | TReprPin(_, _) | TCont(_, _, _, _) | TAlias(_, _) | TValue(_) => (),
}

// The STRUCTURAL belt after a tentative apply: the post-apply handle
// must read NBound (the narrowing stuck without a structural conflict).
// This is NOT the proof — a bind always sticks, so boundness alone once
// admitted an !Alloc claim on an allocating body; the proof is the
// row subsumption apply_annotation_tentatively now returns, conjoined
// with this read at `lock_holds`. Failure modes here:
//   NErrorHole — occurs check or a structural conflict caused
//                inference to record failure on the handle.
//   NFree     — the apply didn't bind (shouldn't happen for a
//                well-formed apply_annotation_tentatively).
//   row-kind  — handle was a row variable; not a narrow target.
// The trail-based rollback restores whatever state the handle held
// before the tentative apply, so this check is non-destructive.
fn verify_after_apply(handle) = match graph_chase(handle) {
  GNode(kind, _) => match kind {
    NBound(_) => true,
    NErrorHole(_) => false,
    NFree(_) => false,
    NRowBound(_) => false,
    NRecordRowBound(_, _) => false,
    NRowFree(_) => false,
  },
}

// The Why at a node, as the chains it is made of: the node's own reason —
// what the node is and where its value comes from — and, when its cell links
// to another, the reason of the cell its type is read from, the links between
// elided. The terminal alone answered why the TYPE is what it is and never
// why this node has it: at a parameter's use it said "int literal". Each
// chain is expanded through the Reason edges it carries (`why_expand`). At a
// declaration the chains of its row follow: one per effect, from the
// declaration to the site that charges it (`effect_entries_of`).
fn why_from_handle(handle) = {
  let t = graph_chase_handle(handle)
  let here = why_expand(graph_reason_at(handle), 0)
  let chains = effect_entries_of(handle)
  if t == handle { [here] ++ chains } else { [here, why_expand(graph_reason_at(t), 0)] ++ chains }
}

// H6: exhaustive Ty enumeration. Only TFun carries an explicit
// row; every other Ty is treated as the pure row (it has no effect
// behavior by itself). Named explicitly so a future Ty whose row
// semantics aren't pure surfaces as a compile error.
fn extract_row(ty) with Pure = match ty {
  TFun(_, _, row) => row,
  TInt => mk_ef_pure(),
  TFloat => mk_ef_pure(),
  TUnit => mk_ef_pure(),
  TByte => mk_ef_pure(),
  TAddr => mk_ef_pure(),
  TList(_) => mk_ef_pure(),
  TTuple(_) => mk_ef_pure(),
  TVar(_) => mk_ef_pure(),
  TName(_, _) => mk_ef_pure(),
  TRecord(_) => mk_ef_pure(),
  TRecordOpen(_, _) => mk_ef_pure(),
  TRefined(_, _) => mk_ef_pure(),
  // resuming a continuation performs its frozen world (the TFun analog: extract_row(TFun) => row)
  TCont(_, _, _, world) => world,
  TAlias(_, inner) => extract_row(inner),
  TReprPin(_, base) => extract_row(base),
  TValue(_) => mk_ef_pure(),
}

// The annotation's source text — what an accept writes, and what a
// teach line names.
fn show_annotation(ann) = match ann {
  APure(_) => "with Pure",
  ANotEffect(name, _) => "with !{name}",
  ARefined(r) => "{r.param}: {r.alias}",
  AReturns(r) => "-> {r.alias}",
}

fn show_capability(cap) = match cap {
  CRealTime => "Real-time safe (proven zero allocation)",
  CIsolated => "Isolated (proven no file or console I/O)",
  CSandbox => "Sandbox (proven no network access)",
  CTrapFree => "Trap-free (nothing it reaches can trap; termination unproven)",
  CPure => "Pure (proven to perform no effect at all)",
  CProvenAbsence(name) => "Proven absence of {name} (the transitive !{name})",
}

// What a suggestion buys, in the developer's words: what it makes safe, what
// that unlocks and what it asks of the callers; or what it unlocks and who
// stands on it. Never the ledger's count — "annotate this and you unlock
// that" is the sentence a person reads (Morgan, 2026-10-27).
fn show_leverage(lev) = match lev {
  Leverage(l) => if l.discharged > 0 {
    "{join(" and ", unique(l.safe))}{unlocked_clause(l.locks)}{refuted_clause(l.refuted)}{opened_clause(l.opened)}"
  } else {
    "unlocks {join(", ", l.locks)}; {stood_on(l.stands)}"
  },
}

fn unlocked_clause(locks) =
  if len(locks) == 0 { "" } else { ", which unlocks {join(" and ", locks)}" }

// The declarations whose clauses stand on a lock, named. A lock is built
// only from its dependents, so the empty case is the record's own word.
fn stood_on(names) = match names {
  [] => "nothing stands on it",
  [one] => "`{one}` stands on it",
  _ => "{join(", ", map(backticked, names))} stand on it",
}

fn backticked(n: String) = "`{n}`"

fn counted(n, noun: String) = if n == 1 { "1 {noun}" } else { "{int_to_str(n)} {noun}s" }

fn refuted_clause(n) = if n == 0 { "" } else { "; {counted(n, "call")} break it and will refuse" }

fn opened_clause(n) = if n == 0 { "" } else { "; {counted(n, "call")} must still show it" }

// THE GRADIENT'S ANSWER AS TEXT — one home for every surface that speaks it
// (the address surface's Teach facet, `mentl teach`, the edit session's
// block): the annotation to add and what it buys; the alternatives a
// question names; the predicate a parameter needs that no declared type
// says.
fn show_teaching(t) = match t {
  TeachAdd(s) => match s {
    AnnotationSuggestion(ann, lev, _) => "add `{show_annotation(ann)}` — {show_leverage(lev)}",
  },
  TeachAsk(ss) => "{join(" or ", map(quoted_annotation, ss))} — either way {tie_leverage(ss)}; they mean different things, so which is meant?",
  TeachNeed(param, pred, sp) => match sp {
    Span(line, _, _, _) => "`{param}` must make {show_predicate(pred)} hold (line {int_to_str(line)}), and no refinement in reach states it — declare one and annotate `{param}`",
  },
  TeachLift(ls) => "lift {join(", ", map(backticked, map(lift_text, ls)))} — the body already fixes {if len(ls) == 1 { "it" } else { "each" }}, so the type adds nothing; a type written here should claim more: a bound, ownership or a width",
  TeachNone => "(no next-step annotation)",
}

fn quoted_annotation(s) = match s {
  AnnotationSuggestion(ann, _, _) => "`{show_annotation(ann)}`",
}

fn tie_leverage(ss) = match ss {
  [] => "",
  [s, ..._] => match s {
    AnnotationSuggestion(_, lev, _) => show_leverage(lev),
  },
}

// THE SECOND WHY RENDERER AND THE SECOND POSITION FANOUT ARE DELETED HERE,
// and what they were is worth one paragraph because a projection with two
// homes is the Carried-Truth Law at the surface the medium exists to serve.
//
// Four decls named render_why, render_why_at, render_leaf and indent_for
// walked a Reason DAG into an indented structural dump — no backticks on
// those, because they no longer resolve and a reference that points nowhere
// is the thing the comment-ref gate exists to refuse. `mentl why` never
// called them: the live renderer is why_lines (src/space.mn, the Why
// facet's hops under the View), which walks the same chain and renders
// file:line through the range map, each hop's
// phrase, the binding's literal value read live from `node_const`, and the
// author's attached `//` lede from `graph_comment_at`. One was a tree of
// constructor names; the other speaks the developer's file back to them.
// Two homes, and the dead one was the worse form — which is how a duplicate
// always ends, because only one of them is being improved.
//
// A decl named teach_session fanned a position across five Teach ops into a
// 5-tuple "IDE panel", and its own comment said it was the "same `<|` shape
// as cursor.mn's eight-aspect fanout". That fanout is the SHIPPED address
// surface (`mentl <file:line>`), so this was a narrower copy of it with
// three of the eight arms missing, reachable from nothing. Its private
// vocabulary — teach_ic_loop, the TeachSession type, caret_span_from_handle,
// annotation_inferred_for — went with it, likewise unbackticked.
//
// Measured by the medium on itself: `mentl query src/main.mn unreachable`
// named all eleven, in one read, at the moment the facet was asked.

